<ChartsRuntimeNotice />
<DocsExample TExample="BarGroupedExample" Title="Grouped vertical bars" Description="Several bar series in one chart sit side by side for each category. Use them to compare a few measures across the same categories." />
<DocsExample TExample="BarHorizontalExample" Title="Horizontal bars" Description="Orientation turns the bars sideways. Use horizontal bars when category names are long or there are many categories." />
<DocsExample TExample="BarHorizontalCompareExample" Title="Horizontal comparison of one measure" Description="Each series draws one bar per category, so a single category with several series compares one measure side by side, such as this quarter against last quarter. A lone category is centered in the plot." />
<DocsExample TExample="BarStackedExample" Title="Stacked segments" Description="SegmentSelector splits each bar into colored segments. Use it to show what a total is made of. The series value is the total for the whole bar." />
<DocsExample TExample="BarNegativeExample" Title="Negative values" Description="Bars grow from zero in both directions, so gains and losses share one axis. Use it for profit and loss or change from a baseline." />
<DocsExample TExample="BarDataLabelsExample" Title="Data labels" Description="ShowDataLabels prints each value on its bar, and DataLabelFormat controls the format. Use it when readers need exact numbers without hovering." />
<DocsExample TExample="BarHorizontalDataLabelsExample" Title="Data labels on horizontal bars" Description="A label that fits inside its bar is centered on it. A label for a short or zero bar is drawn just past the bar end and stays clear of the axis line." />
